WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.020
SBOMs need operational handling after generation.

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:13.320
Teams should decide where SBOMs are stored, which release they belong to, who can access them, how long they are retained, and how updates or corrections are handled.

3
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:22.380
Storing SBOMs with release records helps connect the inventory to the artifact that customers, operators, or internal teams may ask about later.

4
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.280
It also prevents the latest file from being mistaken for every historical release.

5
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.680
Sharing should follow policy and audience needs.

6
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:38.860
Internal SBOMs may include detailed build metadata, supplier information, private package names, or architecture hints.

7
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:46.560
Customer-facing SBOMs may need to be filtered, packaged, signed, or accompanied by support guidance.

8
00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:51.620
Supplier SBOMs may feed internal inventories and require their own review before they are trusted.

9
00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:55.080
Different audiences may need different levels of detail.

10
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.160
Privacy and sensitivity deserve attention.

11
00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:08.340
SBOMs can reveal dependency versions, component choices, supplier relationships, unpatched components, and details about how a product is assembled.

12
00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.680
That does not mean SBOMs should be hidden by default.

13
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:21.580
It means sharing should be intentional, governed, and aligned with security, legal, customer, and operational expectations.

14
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.160
The policy should explain who can approve external sharing requests and timing.

15
00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.760
Governance defines who owns the process.

16
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:37.900
Someone should own generation, quality review, publication, storage, correction, customer response, and updates when dependency data changes.

17
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.080
Good governance makes SBOMs usable during routine release work and during urgent response work.

18
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:51.940
Without ownership, even a technically valid SBOM can become stale, hard to find, or disconnected from decision-making.

19
00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:58.760
Ownership should be documented in the same places teams already use for release responsibilities.
